1. Design Concept
Text
Style & Mood:
Apple-inspired "Liquid Glass" enterprise dashboard built on a cool ice-blue canvas (#eef2fb) with three soft, static navy/cobalt/sky ambient glow blobs behind translucent frosted-white surfaces. The mood is crisp, airy, calm, and high-precision — corporate AkzoNobel navy (#00227A) anchors every primary action and active state, while neutral charcoal ink (#1d1d1f) and muted Apple-style grays (#525257, #86868b) keep dense analytics, tables, and admin workflows effortless to scan.

What Makes It Look Unique:
1. Layered translucency without visual clutter: Only top-level primary surfaces (the floating sticky navigation bar and main section cards) use real backdrop blur + high saturation (`blur(24px) saturate(180%)`), a translucent white fill (`rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.55)`), an inner top highlight (`inset 0 1px 0 rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.6)`), and a 1px diagonal specular gradient border (`135deg`, bright top-left fading to subtle bottom-right).
2. Strict "no nested blur" discipline: Everything inside a glass panel (pills, inputs, inset boxes, table wrappers, dropdown menus) uses translucent or solid white fills with subtle hairline borders and zero `backdrop-filter`, preventing muddiness and keeping performance fast.
3. Floating pill geometry: The sticky header floats with 12px top clearance and 24px corners; all navigation tabs, filter slicer chips, and action buttons are full capsules (`border-radius: 9999px`) that snap to solid deep navy (#00227A) with a soft blue elevation shadow when active.
4. Editorial tabular precision: System Apple / Inter typography paired with monospaced tabular numerals (`.font-mono-num`) for every case number, date, count, and currency value, plus 11px uppercase tracked labels (`tracking-[0.04em]`) for metadata and table headers.
5. Distinct corporate chart palette: Charts stand out cleanly against frosted white using a strict 6-color corporate palette (deep blue, magenta-rose, violet, cyan-blue, plum, royal indigo) that repeats at 60% (and 35% where needed) opacity for additional series.
